        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "08dadf37-bc2c-5240-915f-5d83f521746f",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Glazunov, Marius Petipa, John Landor, Ilya Musin\n- Works: Symphony No. 9 in D minor, Raymonda\n- Organizations: Saint Petersburg Conservatory, Petrograd Conservatory, Leningrad Conservatory, Oxford University, Royal Academy of Music\n- Places: London, Saint Petersburg\n- Events: Bolshevik Revolution\n\nFacts:\n- Alexander Glazunov began composing Symphony No. 9 in D minor in 1910.\n- Alexander Glazunov served as director of the Saint Petersburg Conservatory from 1905 to 1928.\n- Following the Bolshevik Revolution, Alexander Glazunov helped reorganize the Saint Petersburg Conservatory into the Petrograd Conservatory and then the Leningrad Conservatory.\n- Raymonda is a ballet in three acts and four scenes with an apotheosis, choreographed by Marius Petipa, with music by Alexander Glazunov as his Opus 57.\n- Alexander Glazunov's Symphony No. 9 in D minor remained unfinished at his death in 1936.\n- John Landor studied at Oxford University, the Royal Academy of Music in London, and the St Petersburg Conservatory under conducting teacher Ilya Musin.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
